ll,{"style":{"fontSize":"0.72rem","color":"#6a8fa8","fontFamily":"'DM Sans', sans-serif"},"children":["May 28, 2026"," · ","3 min read"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontWeight":900,"fontSize":"clamp(1.8rem, 6vw, 2.5rem)","lineHeight":1.15,"letterSpacing":"-0.025em","color":"#e8f4ff","marginBottom":"1rem"},"children":"Threat Hunting: Finding Attackers Before They Strike"}],["$","p",null,{"style":{"fontSize":"1rem","color":"#3d5a6e","lineHeight":1.7,"fontFamily":"'DM Sans', sans-serif","fontWeight":300},"children":"Proactive detection strategies that shift security teams from reactive to offensive."}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"height":"1px","background":"linear-gradient(90deg, #a78bfa40, transparent)","margin":"2rem 0"}}]]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"prose","style":{"paddingBottom":"5rem"},"children":[["$","p","0",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Threat hunting flips the script — instead of waiting for passive firewall alerts, you actively search your network for signs of compromise. "}}],["$","div","1",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","2",{"children":"Why Passive Defense Fails"}],["$","div","3",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","4",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Most breaches go undetected for over 200 days. Automated tools like standard SIEMs catch known malware signatures, but skilled attackers using \"living off the land\" techniques (like abusing PowerShell or legitimate admin tools) evade them easily. Human-led hunting fills that gap."}}],["$","div","5",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","6",{"children":"The Threat Hunting Lifecycle"}],["$","div","7",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","8",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>1. Form a Hypothesis</strong>"}}],["$","p","9",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"You don't just look around randomly. You start with threat intelligence. For example: \"A new APT group is exploiting a specific zero-day in web servers to drop web shells.\""}}],["$","div","10",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","11",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>2. Collect and Process Data</strong>"}}],["$","p","12",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"You need high-fidelity telemetry. This includes endpoint logs (Windows Event Logs, Sysmon), network flows (PCAP data), and identity logs. "}}],["$","div","13",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","14",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>3. Investigate the Environment</strong>"}}],["$","p","15",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Time to dive into the data. Let's say you suspect an attacker is doing internal reconnaissance. You might query your logs for abnormal network scanning. "}}],["$","div","16",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","17",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"### Practical Scenario: Hunting for Internal Recon"}}],["$","div","18",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","19",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Imagine you are using a Linux environment to audit network logs. An attacker who breached a workstation might use Nmap to map your internal subnets."}}],["$","div","20",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","21",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"You can hunt for this by analyzing Zeek connection logs. A simple command-line hunt might look like this:"}}],["$","div","22",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","23",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<code></code>`bash"}}],["$","p","24",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"# Look for a single internal IP connecting to many different hosts on port 445 (SMB)"}}],["$","p","25",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"cat conn.log | awk '{print $3, $5, $6}' | grep \"445\" | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 10"}}],["$","p","26",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<code></code>`"}}],["$","div","27",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","28",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"If you see one IP address attempting to connect to 200 different machines on port 445 in a span of five minutes, you haven't just found an anomaly — you've likely found an active lateral movement attempt."}}],["$","div","29",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","30",{"children":"Key Hunting Tools"}],["$","div","31",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","ul","32",{"children":["$","li",null,{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>Zeek:</strong> Unmatched for network traffic analysis and extracting protocol metadata."}}]}],["$","ul","33",{"children":["$","li",null,{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>Velociraptor:</strong> Incredible for endpoint visibility and live forensics. You can query thousands of endpoints simultaneously."}}]}],["$","ul","34",{"children":["$","li",null,{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>YARA:</strong> The \"pattern matching Swiss army knife\" for identifying malware families based on hex strings and text patterns."}}]}],["$","div","35",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","36",{"children":"Conclusion"}],["$","div","37",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","38",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"A successful hunt doesn't always end with finding an attacker. Often, a hunt reveals misconfigurations or blind spots in your logging. Document everything you find, patch the holes, and refine your hypothesis for the next hunt."}}]]}],["$","$L9",null,{}]]}]]}]]
